  done","path":"out.txt"}}
{"sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-1f8e87c1…","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"wrot
  e out.txt (4 bytes)"}}]}
{"sessionUpdate":"tool_call","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-1acf6c13…","name":"read_file","title":"Reading","kind":"read","status":"pending","rawInput":{"path":"out.
  txt"}}
{"sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-1acf6c13…","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"<pat
  h>out.txt</path>\n<content>\n1\tdone\n</content>"}}]}
```

What session/load replays for the same session, **three frames** instead of five:

```
{"sessionUpdate":"user_message_chunk","messageId":"0667433c…","content":{"type":"text","text":"Run 'echo hello' in the shell, then write out.txt containing
  'done', then read it back, then summarise in one sentence."}}

{"sessionUpdate":"agent_message_chunk","messageId":"2a9f8e54…","content":{"type":"text","text":"Previous tool execution:\n\nTool shell (failure):\nshell arguments
  must match the advertised action schema\n\nTool shell (failure):\nshell run explicit shell requires tty=true\n\nTool shell
  (success):\n{\"session_id\":null,\"state\":\"completed\",\"backend\":\"tty\",…,\"output_delta\":\"hello\\r\\n\"}\n\nTool write_file (success):\nwrote out.txt (4
  bytes)\n\nTool read_file (success):\n<path>out.txt</path>…"}}

{"sessionUpdate":"agent_message_chunk","messageId":"f813acdd…","content":{"type":"text","text":"'echo hello' printed \"hello\" with exit code 0, out.txt was
  written containing \"done\" (4 bytes) and read back successfully — everything worked as expected."}}
```

What we would expect. The same frames as live, in order so a client can rebuild the exact transcript:

```
  {"sessionUpdate":"user_message_chunk","messageId":"0667433c…","content":{"type":"text","text":"Run 'echo hello' …"}}
  {"sessionUpdate":"tool_call","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-1f8e87c1…","name":"write_file","title":"Writing","kind":"edit","status":"completed","rawInput":{"content"
  :"done","path":"out.txt"},"content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"wrote out.txt (4 bytes)"}}]}
  {"sessionUpdate":"tool_call","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-1acf6c13…","name":"read_file","title":"Reading","kind":"read","status":"completed","rawInput":{"path":"ou
  t.txt"},"content":[…]}
  {"sessionUpdate":"agent_message_chunk","messageId":"f813acdd…","content":{"type":"text","text":"'echo hello' printed \"hello\" …"}}
```

## Summary

Bifrost (an OpenAI-compatible LLM gateway, maxim-ai/bifrost) fails immediately with fx's `openai-chat-completions` custom connection: every request errors with `fx: InvalidChunk` and the turn aborts (`event=stream_error err=InvalidChunk recovery=stop retry=false`).

The cause is a strict-parsing mismatch: Bifrost emits spec-legal but loosely-populated fields in its SSE chunks that fx's parser rejects:

1. **`"model": ""` in every chunk.** Bifrost streams `"model":""` in each `chat.completion.chunk` (and omits echoing the model back at all). fx's chunk parser requires a non-empty model string and fails the whole stream. Verified by fixing the field through a local normalizing proxy: with `j["model"] = <requested model>` applied to empty values, the identical stream completes and fx prints the model reply (`ok9`, `ok11`, `ok12` across runs).

2. **Nonstandard reasoning delta fields.** Bifrost also emits `reasoning`, `reasoning_content`, and `reasoning_details: [{"type":"reasoning.text","text":...,"signature":...}]` deltas, including a final delta carrying only `reasoning_details` with a bare `signature` and no text. fx's accepted shape appears to be `{"type":"reasoning","text":...}`. Stripping `reasoning_details` alongside the model fix also produced clean runs, so this is a secondary compatibility friction (unknown which of the two fx objects to).

## Reproduction

fx 0.0.13 (mise, macOS arm64), Bifrost 2.x serving `zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash` on `http://localhost:8317/v1`:

```json
{
  "provider": "bifrost",
  "providers": {
    "bifrost": {
      "protocol": "openai-chat-completions",
      "base_url": "http://localhost:8317/v1",
      "auth": { "type": "bearer", "env": "BIFROST_API_KEY" },
      "model_metadata": {
        "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash": {
          "context_window": 204800,
          "max_output_tokens": 32768,
          "supports_tool_use": true
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "models": { "bifrost": "zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash" }
}
```

`fx ask "say hi"` → `fx: InvalidChunk`, exit 1, zero content delivered. Raw SSE from the same endpoint via curl streams fine end to end (role delta → reasoning deltas → content deltas → `finish_reason:stop` → `data: [DONE]`).

The `InvalidChunk` fires on the **first chunk**, which is `{"delta":{"role":"assistant"},"model":"","created":0,"usage":null,...}` — `model` empty and `created` 0.

## What the server sends (trimmed)

```
data: {"id":"msg_...","choices":[{"index":0,"finish_reason":null,"delta":{"role":"assistant"}}],"created":0,"model":"","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null,...}
data: {"id":"msg_...","choices":[{"index":0,"finish_reason":null,"delta":{"reasoning":"The","reasoning_details":[{"index":0,"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The"}],"reasoning_content":"The"}}],"created":0,"model":"","object":"chat.completion.chunk",...}
...
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"finish_reason":"stop","delta":{}}],"created":0,"model":"","object":"chat.completion.chunk",...}
data: [DONE]
```

## Minimal fixes that would make fx compatible

Any one of these, in order of preference:

1. **Default empty `model` to the requested model id** when parsing `chat.completion.chunk` (mirrors what #1144 suggests reporting: the served model). One coercion in the chunk decoder.
2. Tolerate `model: ""` / missing `model` in chunk parsing (strict-optional rather than strict-required).
3. Accept (or ignore) `reasoning_details` entries with unknown `type` values such as `reasoning.text`, and tolerate signature-only trailing reasoning deltas, so gateways that emit them don't poison the stream.

Optionally, surfacing the offending raw chunk (redacted) in the `stream_error` event would make reports like this one self-diagnosing — the current `err=InvalidChunk` carries no chunk context and took a proxy-in-the-middle experiment to isolate.

## Related

- #1144 covers the adjacent model-echo discrepancy on custom connections (requested vs served id). This report is about the parse failure when the served id is empty.

### Issue details

I think I stumbled upon two configuration bugs:

- When an MCP server is using an `http://` URL without a port (e.g., `http://localhost/mcp`), fx treats that as an invalid URL instead defaulting to port 80.
- When user-profile configuration contains an "invalid" MCP server entry, the entire user-profile configuration is rejected.  (In contrast, project-level configuration will skip the invalid entry, and still connect to the other MCP servers.)

So, for example, the following user-profile configuration will connect to neither MCP server:

```json
{
  "mcp": {
    "remote-server": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.example.com/mcp"
    },
    "local-server": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://localhost/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

